issues.problematic_code_snippet:
if (ret == AOF_OPEN_ERR || ret == AOF_FAILED || ret == AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED) {
    goto cleanup;
}

findings.body:
When the base AOF is in RDB-preamble format, `loadSingleAppendOnlyFile()` loads the preamble and then only calls `loadingAbsProgress(ftello(fp))` at ::file{file="src/aof.c"} line 1550. It never assigns `valid_up_to`, so it stays at its initial value of `0`. If the first bytes after the preamble are not `*` (a zero-filled tail after a power loss is the typical case), execution reaches `fmterr`. There, `sb.st_size - valid_up_to` equals the whole file size.

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1725-1728"}

If the total file is under 4096 bytes, the check passes and `truncate(aof_filepath, 0)` runs. The preamble is erased, the server logs "AOF loaded anyway", and it starts empty. The same happens for any file whose first command fails to parse.

This is most likely with `aof-use-rdb-preamble yes`, which is the default. Small datasets make the preamble easy to stay under the threshold.

::callout{type="security"}
This is silent data loss on startup. The operator gets a log line, not an error. The issue is opt-in, but the failure mode is total and not recoverable from the file itself.

::callout{type="warning"}
Set `valid_up_to` after a successful RDB preamble load (for example, `valid_up_to = ftello(fp)` right after `rdbLoadRio` succeeds). Also refuse to truncate when `valid_up_to == 0` and the file is in RDB format, or treat `valid_up_to == 0` as "nothing to salvage" and fail the load instead of truncating.

The truncation in `fmterr:` runs inside `loadSingleAppendOnlyFile()`. The caller only decides afterwards that a non-last file is fatal. In `loadAppendOnlyFiles()`, the base path converts `AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED` to `AOF_FAILED` at ::file{file="src/aof.c"} line 1838, and the incr path does the same at line 1872.

::code{file="src/aof.c" lines="1836-1842"}

So the sequence is: a corrupt middle incr file is truncated on disk, the server logs "Fatal error: the truncated file is not the last file", and exits. The operator then has a file that has already lost its tail, and the error message does not say so. A rerun with a different manifest order or a manual `redis-check-aof` now works from already-mutated input.

The existing `aof-load-truncated` path has the same ordering problem. This PR makes it reachable for generic format errors, not only EOF.

::callout{type="warning"}
The fix is to defer the truncation until the caller knows the file is the last one. One option is to pass `last_file` into `loadSingleAppendOnlyFile()` and only truncate when it is true. Another is to return `AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED` without truncating and let `loadAppendOnlyFiles()` perform the truncate after the last-file check.

At the cleanup label, `loadAppendOnlyFiles()` calls `stopLoading(ret == AOF_OK || ret == AOF_TRUNCATED)` at ::file{file="src/aof.c"} line 1894. The new `AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED` result is not in that list. When a last base or incr file is recovered, `stopLoading(0)` fires `REDISMODULE_SUBEVENT_LOADING_FAILED` even though the server is about to serve data.

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1893-1895"}

Modules that listen for the loading events will see a failure for a successful startup.

`wait_for_log_messages` in `tests/support/util.tcl` returns as soon as any single pattern matches. The first new test at ::file{file="tests/integration/aof.tcl"} lines 714-720 lists two patterns, "AOF loaded anyway because aof-load-broken is enabled" and "Fatal error: the truncated file is not the last file", and the test title suggests both matter. Either line alone satisfies it, so the test passes even if one of them is wrong.

The "Intermediate AOF is broken" test at lines 817-823 has the same issue and only checks the fatal line. It never checks that a truncation actually happened. Combined with the swap at lines 825-829, the final assertion `get fo == "mid"` passes whether or not the mid file was truncated. The test does not verify recovery.

::code{file="tests/integration/aof.tcl" lines="714-721"}
